\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces News-driven forecast adjustment around 5 March 2024. The upper panel compares 4-hour annualized realized volatility with TS-only and NED forecasts. The numbered vertical markers identify the three articles with the largest remaining signals at 18:30 UTC. The lower panel stacks their learned signals, $s_k\qopname  \relax o{exp}(-\lambda _k\Delta t_k)$, and overlays the sum for all retained articles in the twelve-hour lookback. The dashed vertical line marks the selection time. The signals are inputs to the residual map, rather than additive contributions to annualized volatility.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:casestudy}{{3}{18}{News-driven forecast adjustment around 5 March 2024. The upper panel compares 4-hour annualized realized volatility with TS-only and NED forecasts. The numbered vertical markers identify the three articles with the largest remaining signals at 18:30 UTC. The lower panel stacks their learned signals, $s_k\exp (-\lambda _k\Delta t_k)$, and overlays the sum for all retained articles in the twelve-hour lookback. The dashed vertical line marks the selection time. The signals are inputs to the residual map, rather than additive contributions to annualized volatility.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Joint distribution of learned article magnitude $s$ and decay rate $\lambda $ in the BTC 4-hour models. Each point is one of 7,845 articles entering an out-of-sample forecast; the marginal distributions appear above and to the right. Decay rates are measured per minute and shown on a logarithmic axis. Larger magnitudes tend to be associated with slower decay.\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:joint}{{4}{19}{Joint distribution of learned article magnitude $s$ and decay rate $\lambda $ in the BTC 4-hour models. Each point is one of 7,845 articles entering an out-of-sample forecast; the marginal distributions appear above and to the right. Decay rates are measured per minute and shown on a logarithmic axis. Larger magnitudes tend to be associated with slower decay.\relax }{figure.caption.9}{}}
